There is no code shown that guarantees `map` is always non‑null. In fact:

- `new Environment(map)` is called in `GameWorld.getEnvironment(IMap map)` even when `map` originates from outside the class; that caller already checks for `map == null`, but nothing in `Environment` prevents other call sites or constructors (including the parameterless `this()` used inside) from leaving `map` as `null`.
- `GameWorld.loadEnvironment(Environment env)` explicitly allows `env` to be `null`, and `GameWorld.unloadEnvironment()` sets `this.environment = null`; there is no global guarantee that any `Environment` instance held elsewhere must always have a non‑null `map`.
- Multiple methods in `Environment` itself defensively check `this.getMap() != null` before using it, e.g.:
  ```java
  if (this.getMap() != null) {
    this.loadMapObjects();
    this.addStaticShadows();
    this.addAmbientLight();
  }
